1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,522
Inside every cell, tiny molecular machines flip at random between a few shapes, called

2
00:00:06,522 --> 00:00:10,920
states. Here is the simplest kind: three states in a ring.

3
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:16,017
Now turn a knob, so that one kind of hop happens a little more often.

4
00:00:16,017 --> 00:00:19,025
How does the time spent in each state shift?

5
00:00:19,025 --> 00:00:21,212
And how far could it ever shift?

6
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:25,465
That matters for how sharply a cell can sense a signal.

7
00:00:25,465 --> 00:00:29,893
In 2026, the physicists Fancher and Horowitz guessed an answer.

8
00:00:29,893 --> 00:00:35,304
We prove it for a whole family of networks, and find where our method breaks.

9
00:00:36,291 --> 00:00:42,641
The arrows are possible hops. A rate says how often a hop tends to happen while you occupy

10
00:00:42,641 --> 00:00:48,568
its starting state. Clockwise hops have rate two per second; anticlockwise hops have

11
00:00:48,568 --> 00:00:49,203
rate one.

12
00:00:49,591 --> 00:00:57,329
Drop a dot on state 0 and let it hop. It waits a random time, then jumps, clockwise twice

13
00:00:57,329 --> 00:00:58,807
as often as back.

14
00:00:59,191 --> 00:01:05,607
Time each state with a stopwatch. The fractions jump around at first, then settle, here

15
00:01:05,607 --> 00:01:11,875
at one third each. These long-run fractions are the steady state: pi zero, pi one and

16
00:01:11,875 --> 00:01:12,391
pi two.

17
00:01:12,758 --> 00:01:18,974
Steady does not mean still: the dot keeps circling clockwise, like a tiny motor.

18
00:01:19,955 --> 00:01:23,654
Can you predict the steady state without simulating?

19
00:01:23,654 --> 00:01:29,915
Yes, with trees. A tree pointing at a chosen root gives every other state one arrow out,

20
00:01:29,915 --> 00:01:33,971
so that following arrows from anywhere leads to the root.

21
00:01:34,355 --> 00:01:39,903
On a ring this is easy. Cut one edge, then point everything else towards the root.

22
00:01:39,903 --> 00:01:43,355
Three edges to cut means three trees for each root.

23
00:01:43,721 --> 00:01:47,044
A tree's weight is the product of its rates.

24
00:01:47,044 --> 00:01:51,575
For root 0, two clockwise arrows give 2 times 2, which is 4.

25
00:01:51,575 --> 00:01:55,577
One of each gives 2. Two anticlockwise arrows give 1.

26
00:01:56,555 --> 00:01:59,652
Now the trick, called the Markov chain tree theorem.

27
00:01:59,652 --> 00:02:04,776
The time spent in a state is proportional to the total weight of the trees pointing at

28
00:02:04,776 --> 00:02:04,955
it.

29
00:02:05,355 --> 00:02:11,031
Every root has trees weighing 4, 2 and 1, so every total is 7.

30
00:02:11,031 --> 00:02:16,707
Seven out of twenty-one is one third, as the simulation found.

31
00:02:17,088 --> 00:02:22,843
Divide each weight by its total, and each root gets a lottery: heavier trees are more

32
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:28,056
likely. For root 0, the odds are four sevenths, two sevenths and one seventh.

33
00:02:29,055 --> 00:02:34,336
Now turn the knob. Make the hop from 0 to 1 one percent faster.

34
00:02:34,336 --> 00:02:40,455
Only trees that use that arrow notice, and each gets one percent heavier.

35
00:02:40,855 --> 00:02:49,008
Root 0's total stays 7, root 1's becomes 7.06, and root 2's 7.04.

36
00:02:49,008 --> 00:02:59,671
So pi zero falls about 0.47 percent, pi one rises 0.38 percent, and pi two about 0.1.

37
00:03:00,055 --> 00:03:05,645
For a tiny percentage nudge, the relative response is each root's share using the arrow,

38
00:03:05,645 --> 00:03:10,536
zero, six sevenths and four sevenths, minus their average, ten twenty-firsts.

39
00:03:10,536 --> 00:03:13,903
That gives minus ten, eight and two, over twenty-one.

40
00:03:14,888 --> 00:03:18,185
Fancher and Horowitz saw this another way.

41
00:03:18,185 --> 00:03:22,267
Pick one tree for every root at once: a tree family.

42
00:03:22,267 --> 00:03:27,056
Three roots with three trees each make twenty-seven families.

43
00:03:27,455 --> 00:03:33,985
Score a family: for each root, 1 if its tree uses the nudged arrow, 0 if not, minus the

44
00:03:33,985 --> 00:03:39,015
average. This family scores minus two thirds, one third, one third.

45
00:03:39,015 --> 00:03:40,967
That's its building block.

46
00:03:41,355 --> 00:03:47,397
Draw each root's tree from its own lottery, independently, and average the building blocks.

47
00:03:47,397 --> 00:03:49,323
You get exactly the response.

48
00:03:50,221 --> 00:03:54,322
Here the three numbers add to zero, so two are enough to plot.

49
00:03:54,322 --> 00:03:58,754
A weighted average always lands inside the shape around its points.

50
00:03:58,754 --> 00:04:03,781
The twenty-seven families land on four points, and the response sits inside.

51
00:04:04,155 --> 00:04:09,111
Now the subtle part. Tip all the arrows of a family into one bag.

52
00:04:09,111 --> 00:04:14,067
Different families can fill exactly the same bag, like these two.

53
00:04:14,455 --> 00:04:18,110
Twenty-seven families make only eighteen different bags.

54
00:04:18,110 --> 00:04:23,135
A family whose bag no other family can make is called uniquely constructable.

55
00:04:23,135 --> 00:04:25,159
Eleven of the twenty-seven are.

56
00:04:25,555 --> 00:04:28,396
The guess: look-alikes are never needed.

57
00:04:28,396 --> 00:04:34,648
For every network, every set of rates and every knob, the response is a weighted average

58
00:04:34,648 --> 00:04:38,059
of uniquely constructable building blocks alone.

59
00:04:38,455 --> 00:04:43,709
Here, that throws away this corner, which only look-alikes reach, so the limit on how

60
00:04:43,709 --> 00:04:46,430
strongly a network can respond gets tighter.

61
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:50,263
They proved it for three states and asked for a general proof.

62
00:04:51,255 --> 00:04:57,280
So which weights? The independent lotteries won't do: forty-four percent of the time,

63
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:58,911
they pick a look-alike.

64
00:04:59,288 --> 00:05:04,415
But look at the score again. Root 1's part depends only on root 1's tree.

65
00:05:04,415 --> 00:05:10,736
So the average only cares about each root's own lottery, not how the lotteries are linked.

66
00:05:11,121 --> 00:05:16,448
The goal: one shared lottery that picks a whole family, gives every root its correct

67
00:05:16,448 --> 00:05:18,857
odds, and never lands on a look-alike.

68
00:05:18,857 --> 00:05:22,281
Pause and think: how could the roots share their luck?

69
00:05:24,455 --> 00:05:27,282
On a ring, a tree is just the edge you cut.

70
00:05:27,282 --> 00:05:32,278
Slide the cut one step, and one arrow flips from clockwise to anticlockwise.

71
00:05:32,278 --> 00:05:35,303
The weight halves, whichever root you look at.

72
00:05:35,688 --> 00:05:43,154
So every tree weight sits on one staircase: one, a half, a quarter, an eighth, a sixteenth.

73
00:05:43,154 --> 00:05:49,800
Root 0 reads the first three steps, root 1 the next window, root 2 the one after.

74
00:05:50,188 --> 00:05:53,018
Each window covers every edge exactly once.

75
00:05:53,018 --> 00:05:58,679
And once round the ring, the staircase drops by a factor of eight, because the ring is

76
00:05:58,679 --> 00:05:59,140
driven.

77
00:06:00,121 --> 00:06:06,516
Now a classic way to run a lottery. Draw each option as a bar whose height counts doublings

78
00:06:06,516 --> 00:06:12,560
of its weight. Add a random bonus from the lopsided Gumbel curve, and pick the tallest

79
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:12,841
bar.

80
00:06:13,221 --> 00:06:17,149
The winner's chances are exactly proportional to weight.

81
00:06:17,149 --> 00:06:22,269
Repeat it for root 0, and the tally settles at four, two and one seventh.

82
00:06:23,155 --> 00:06:29,468
Here's the idea. Give each edge one bonus token, and put that same token on every bar

83
00:06:29,468 --> 00:06:31,771
for that edge, in every window.

84
00:06:32,155 --> 00:06:37,641
Each window holds every edge once, so each root sees three independent bonuses and gets

85
00:06:37,641 --> 00:06:41,803
exactly the right odds. Yet all three roots share the same tokens.

86
00:06:42,688 --> 00:06:47,947
Run it many times. Only seven families ever appear, each with probability exactly one

87
00:06:47,947 --> 00:06:51,040
seventh, and all seven are uniquely constructable.

88
00:06:51,921 --> 00:06:57,327
Their building blocks sit on three points: one family here, two here, four here.

89
00:06:57,327 --> 00:07:02,529
Weigh each family one seventh, and the balance point is exactly the response.

90
00:07:03,521 --> 00:07:08,449
A bonus on the doubling scale multiplies a weight by a positive number.

91
00:07:08,449 --> 00:07:11,086
Call the three multipliers a, b and c.

92
00:07:11,086 --> 00:07:15,041
Divide both hopping rates on each edge by its multiplier.

93
00:07:15,421 --> 00:07:21,753
A tree missing edge a uses edges b and c, so its new weight is its old weight divided

94
00:07:21,753 --> 00:07:27,637
by b times c. Rewrite that as old weight times a, divided by a times b times c.

95
00:07:28,421 --> 00:07:34,882
The denominator is the same for every cut, so choosing the heaviest tree is exactly choosing

96
00:07:34,882 --> 00:07:38,885
the token winner. The same new rates work for every root.

97
00:07:38,885 --> 00:07:40,781
Ties have probability zero.

98
00:07:41,155 --> 00:07:46,726
A family's weight is the product of everything in its bag, so a look-alike would weigh

99
00:07:46,726 --> 00:07:52,104
exactly the same. But somewhere it uses a strictly lighter tree, so it weighs less.

100
00:07:52,104 --> 00:07:53,011
Contradiction.

101
00:07:53,988 --> 00:08:01,051
Rings of any size work the same way. Now join rings at single states and add branches:

102
00:08:01,051 --> 00:08:04,500
a cactus, whose loops never share an edge.

103
00:08:04,888 --> 00:08:10,560
Every tree splits into one piece per loop, pointing towards the global root, plus forced

104
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:15,008
branches. The weights multiply, so the lotteries split independently.

105
00:08:15,008 --> 00:08:19,456
Give each loop its own tokens, glue the picks, and the guess follows.

106
00:08:20,455 --> 00:08:27,136
Now four states, all joined. Each root has sixteen trees, giving over sixty-five thousand

107
00:08:27,136 --> 00:08:32,767
families, but only three hundred and forty-six uniquely constructable ones.

108
00:08:33,155 --> 00:08:39,645
For one set of rates, label each of the sixty-four root and tree pairs with plus or minus

109
00:08:39,645 --> 00:08:44,459
one. Every uniquely constructable family scores zero, two or four.

110
00:08:44,855 --> 00:08:49,873
But averaged over each root's own lottery, the total is minus 0.0566.

111
00:08:49,873 --> 00:08:55,982
A shared lottery would give the same average, for the same reason as before, but its

112
00:08:55,982 --> 00:08:59,036
scores are never negative. So none exists.

113
00:08:59,036 --> 00:09:01,655
This was checked in exact fractions.

114
00:09:02,021 --> 00:09:06,907
For connected networks with hops allowed both ways on every link, the block spreads.

115
00:09:06,907 --> 00:09:12,084
If deleting links or shortening paths gives these four fully joined states, some positive

116
00:09:12,084 --> 00:09:13,829
rates admit no shared lottery.

117
00:09:14,221 --> 00:09:20,302
This does not refute the guess: matching the response asks for less than matching every

118
00:09:20,302 --> 00:09:26,244
tree lottery. One hundred and fifty searches on four states found no violation, but a

119
00:09:26,244 --> 00:09:27,781
search is not a proof.

120
00:09:28,755 --> 00:09:34,761
So, proved by a written argument: the guess holds for every cactus network, of any size,

121
00:09:34,761 --> 00:09:40,562
with any rates. The four-state block is an exact computer certificate, and its spread

122
00:09:40,562 --> 00:09:41,859
is a written proof.

123
00:09:42,255 --> 00:09:48,478
These are research drafts, not externally refereed or formally checked in proof software.

124
00:09:48,478 --> 00:09:54,351
Correctness confidence is high, while originality remains less certain: the campaign

125
00:09:54,351 --> 00:09:58,407
search found no earlier result, but cannot prove priority.

126
00:09:59,388 --> 00:10:05,471
To recap. One: the steady state comes from weighted tree totals, and its response is

127
00:10:05,471 --> 00:10:07,644
an average over tree families.

128
00:10:08,021 --> 00:10:13,964
Two: on a ring, one token per edge gives every root the right odds at once, and never

129
00:10:13,964 --> 00:10:19,277
a look-alike. Three: four linked states block the method, but not the guess.

130
00:10:19,655 --> 00:10:24,083
Still open in the paper: is the guess true for every network?

131
00:10:24,083 --> 00:10:29,527
The smallest uncovered graph is the diamond, two triangles sharing an edge.

132
00:10:29,527 --> 00:10:34,319
One token per edge fails there; it needs a different construction.
